12:\subsection{Static Semantics}\label{basic-static}
13:A static semantics for \basiccore\ is provided in
14:Figures~\ref{fig:basic-static1}, \ref{fig:basic-static2}, and
15:\ref{fig:basic-static3}.
16:The \basiccore\ static semantics is based on the static
17:semantics of Featherweight Generic Java (FGJ)~\cite{fgj}. The major
18:difference is the division of classes into traits and objects. 
19:Both trait and object definitions
20:include method definitions but only object definitions include field
21:definitions. With traits, \basiccore\ supports multiple inheritance.
22:However, due to the similarity of traits and objects, many of the rules
23:in the \basiccore\ dynamic and static semantics combine
24:the two cases.  Note that \basiccore\ allows parametric 
25:polymorphism, subtype polymorphism, and overriding in much the
26:same way that FGJ does.
